# ADDED 2026-08-13 — surfaced from the "How to Think About the Future"
# series highlights batch (Parts 2-6, MJSullivan's own manual show-notes
# transcription), same triage/verification discipline as the Frankly_138
# pass. See docs/sidecar-cleanup-handoff.md for the full session entry.
#############################################

tgs:Concept.TheSenecaEffect a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.clubofrome.org/publication/the-seneca-effect-2017/> ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Seneca Effect"@en ;
    skos:definition "The observation that complex systems tend to grow slowly but collapse rapidly — named after the Roman philosopher Seneca's own observation that fortune grows slowly but ruin is swift. Developed by Ugo Bardi as a general model for why decline, once it starts, tends to be much faster than the growth that preceded it."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.UgoBardi .

tgs:Concept.LandscapeOfPossibleFutures a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com> ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Landscape of Possible Futures"@en ;
    skos:definition "Hagens' own application of the landscape metaphor (valleys, ridges, switchbacks, erosion) to civilizational futures — see tgs:Monologue.Frankly_145_Part3UphillFutures for the full framework. Adapts Conrad Waddington's epigenetic landscape (originally a model of cellular differentiation) to a different domain entirely — a genuine reframing, not a direct reuse, hence HagensConcept despite the explicit external inspiration."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.ConradWaddington .

tgs:Concept.PerpetualMulligans a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com> ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    skos:prefLabel "Perpetual Mulligans"@en ;
    skos:definition "Hagens' own framing for how cheap, abundant fossil energy has functioned historically as an economic 'do-over' — letting mistakes, inefficiencies, and bad decisions get absorbed and corrected via sheer energy surplus rather than genuine adaptation, a buffer that thins as energy surplus declines."@en .

tgs:Concept.UnicornStrategies a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com> ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Unicorn Strategies"@en ;
    skos:definition "Hagens' own term for actions that are good moves regardless of which of the 4 composite future Scenarios (see tgs:Scenario) actually comes to pass — genuinely robust across radically different futures rather than a bet on one specific outcome. Named examples from the source episode: ocean conservation, nuclear disarmament, indigenous language preservation."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Deliberately NOT formally linked to thinkr:Scenario via any property — the relationship (robust across all 4 named Scenarios) is real but this session's own 'don't over-link prematurely' discipline applies; noted here in prose instead."@en .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — RR-26 "Why Community Fails" bootstrap.
#############################################

tgs:Concept.Communing a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com> ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Communing"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nora Bateson's framing of 'communing' as the necessary precursor to 'community' — the practice of genuine mutual relating (giving before being owed, radical hospitality, tending to the whole as a way of tending to the self) that most attempts at community-building skip past by going straight to shared values, organizing, and logistics."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NoraBateson .

tgs:Concept.Symmathesy a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dcterms:source <https://journals.isss.org/index.php/proceedings59th/article/view/2720> ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Symmathesy"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nora Bateson's 2015 coinage (Greek 'sym' + 'mathesis,' together + learning) for an entity composed through contextual mutual learning by interaction — a living system (a family, an ecosystem, a conversation) that is genuinely shaped by its own internal relationships and interdependencies, not just a collection of independently-acting parts."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NoraBateson ;
    skos:scopeNote "dcterms:source UPGRADED 2026-08-13 from a generic WordPress blog post to the real ISSS (International Society for the Systems Sciences) academic conference proceedings — a strictly more authoritative citation for this same 2015 coinage, surfaced via RR-26's own real linked show notes."@en .

tgs:Concept.Schismogenesis a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com> ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Schismogenesis"@en ;
    skos:definition "Gregory Bateson's term (from his 1930s anthropological fieldwork) for a process in which relationships escalate through cumulative interaction — either symmetrically (both parties responding to each other's same behavior with more of the same, e.g. an arms race) or complementarily (parties responding with opposite behaviors that reinforce each other, e.g. dominance and submission), each pattern intensifying over time unless deliberately interrupted."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.GregoryBateson .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — Berman 5-interview batch (TGS_101).
#############################################

tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com> ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Maximum Power Principle"@en ;
    skos:definition "Howard T. Odum's principle (building on Alfred Lotka's earlier work) that self-organizing systems tend to develop and prevail when they maximize their intake and useful transformation of energy, not merely their efficiency — a foundational idea in ecological energetics directly connected to EROI (Charles Hall, a notable Odum student, is a central figure in that literature too)."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.HowardOdum .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — surfaced via TGS_10's own real linked show notes.
#############################################

tgs:Concept.KleibersLaw a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dcterms:source <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kleiber%27s_law> ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Kleiber's Law"@en ;
    skos:definition "Max Kleiber's 1932 empirical finding that an organism's basal metabolic rate scales to roughly the 3/4 power of its body mass, not the 2/3 power a simple surface-area argument would predict — one of the few genuine quantitative laws in biology, still without a fully settled theoretical explanation, and used in this graph's own source material as a real biological analogy for economic/systemic energy-scaling arguments."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.MaxKleiber .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — RR-26 Tier 2/4 enrichment, from its own real
# linked show notes.
#############################################

tgs:Concept.RadicalHospitality a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com> ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Radical Hospitality"@en ;
    skos:definition "A practice of genuine, unscripted welcome — extended both to others and to oneself — treated in this graph's source material as a necessary ingredient for real community, distinct from the transactional, role-bound interactions that ordinarily substitute for it. No single originator credited in the source material (etymological, not attributional, citations given for both words) — modeled as umbrella Concept, not thinkr:HagensConcept."@en .

tgs:Concept.Amonging a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.combining.life/> ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Amonging"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nora Bateson's own neologism for the edge of the self as genuinely porous and relational rather than fixed — being fundamentally constituted 'among' one's relationships and context, not separately from them. Directly connects to both her book Combining and her newer book Belly (whose own publisher description uses the phrase 'the among-ing of all life') — not confidently attributable to one specific book over the other from the sources checked."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.NoraBateson .

tgs:Concept.DunbarsNumber a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dcterms:source <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dunbar%27s_number> ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Dunbar's Number"@en ;
    skos:definition "Robin Dunbar's proposed cognitive limit (roughly 150) on the number of people with whom one can maintain genuinely stable social relationships, derived from a correlation between primate brain/neocortex size and social group size — used in this graph's source material as evidence for prioritizing depth over breadth in relationships."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.RobinDunbar .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — Concept.DarkTriad, previously discussed
# substantively across RR-19, RR-20, and RR-26 but never itself
# formally minted, closing a real gap surfaced while processing
# Frankly_85. Concept.LightTriad, Nate's own explicit antonym framing
# for it, surfaced via Frankly_85 directly.
#
# ARCHITECTURAL RESOLUTION, same day: this is the first real case
# testing whether thinkr:AlternateTermType.Antonym (built 2026-08-13,
# earlier this session, flagged then as unresolved and NEVER actually
# used on any real individual since) or the already-existing
# thinkr:contrastsWith is the right mechanism for a genuine antonym.
# RESOLVED: contrastsWith wins, clearly — Light Triad and Dark Triad
# are NOT two names for the same referent (which is what AlternateTerm
# is for, e.g. EROI/"Energy Return on Investment"); they are two
# DISTINCT, separately-defined constructs that stand in opposition.
# Confirmed independently by the very paper that established this
# contrast: Kaufman et al.'s own 2019 title is literally "Contrasting
# Two Very Different Profiles of Human Nature." contrastsWith's own
# existing definition ("useful tension... does not imply influence in
# either direction") fits this case exactly. See
# thinkr:AlternateTermType.Antonym's own scopeNote for the formal
# resolution note.
#############################################

tgs:Concept.DarkTriad a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0191886902001459> ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Dark Triad"@en ;
    skos:definition "A cluster of three socially aversive personality traits — narcissism, Machiavellianism, and psychopathy — originally proposed by Delroy Paulhus and Kevin Williams (2002). A recurring analytical lens across this graph's source material for understanding how individuals with these traits gain disproportionate influence over institutions and culture, distinct from mere individual pathology."@en ;
    thinkr:contrastsWith tgs:Concept.LightTriad ;
    .

tgs:Concept.LightTriad a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dcterms:source <https://doaj.org/article/6a8567c65a0a46cbb579e2a93b07acd0> ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:prefLabel "Light Triad"@en ;
    skos:definition "Scott Barry Kaufman's proposed counterpart to the Dark Triad (2019, with David Bryce Yaden, Elizabeth Hyde, and Eli Tsukayama) — a cluster of three prosocial traits: Kantianism (treating people as ends in themselves, not means), humanism (belief in the inherent dignity of others), and faith in humanity (a basic trust that most people are good). Hagens' own explicit framing treats it as a real but structurally weaker force than the Dark Triad — a value-based orientation up against trait-based behavior, requiring deliberate group-level support (not just individual goodness) to hold its own."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.ScottBarryKaufman ;
    thinkr:contrastsWith tgs:Concept.DarkTriad ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — RR-2 bootstrap.
#############################################

tgs:Concept.EcologicalFootprint a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dcterms:source <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ecological_footprint> ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ecological Footprint"@en ;
    skos:definition "A quantitative sustainability metric, originated and co-developed by William Rees and his then-PhD-student Mathis Wackernagel (introduced in their 1996 book 'Our Ecological Footprint'), expressing human demand on ecosystems as the amount of biologically productive land and water area required to produce what a population consumes and absorb its waste — the primary tool used to estimate the extent of humanity's ecological overshoot."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.WilliamRees, tgs:Persona.MathisWackernagel .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — the overall action framework from Frankly_132
# "What to Do as the World Falls Apart," which the episode's own
# transcript explicitly labels "a first public draft."
#############################################

tgs:Concept.FrameworkForAction a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Framework for Action"@en ;
    skos:definition "Hagens' own real, explicitly-labeled 'first public draft' framework for what to actually do in response to the more-than-human predicament — introduced in Frankly_132, gestated over roughly a decade and organized under the pressure of real, live world events (the 2026 Iran war/Strait of Hormuz crisis). Structured as 4 levels: a personal foundation of inner work, 'Finding the Others' (trusted networks and shared vocabulary), 6 interdependent thinkr:InterventionFront individuals, and a thinkr:ResponsePhase timeline axis of 3 genuinely overlapping (not strictly sequential) phases that cuts across all 6 Fronts."@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/132-what-to-do-as-the-world-falls-apart> ;
    thinkr:hasFront 
        tgs:InterventionFront.Infrastructure, 
        tgs:InterventionFront.PovertyAndDisplacement, 
        tgs:InterventionFront.EcologicalInterventions,
        tgs:InterventionFront.CivicResilienceAndGovernance, 
        tgs:InterventionFront.CultureAndMeaning, 
        tgs:InterventionFront.EconomicTransition ;
    thinkr:hasPhase tgs:ResponsePhase.StabilityWindow, tgs:ResponsePhase.BendNotBreak, tgs:ResponsePhase.StableAttractor ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct evidence for the sequencing/parallel-construction stance, worth preserving: Hagens explicitly engages with the anti-capitalist/decolonial critique that structural transformation must precede any other intervention, states real sympathy for it, but argues explicitly for building new structures while the old ones still stand rather than waiting for a precondition that would mean 'waiting forever.'"@en .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — a real, cross-episode pattern MJSullivan
# identified and asked to be captured explicitly, synthesized from
# independently-recorded episodes across different guests and years —
# NOT one single named/branded framework Hagens has given one
# consistent term to himself the way Carbon Pulse or the Great
# Simplification are, so modeled as thinkr:Concept only, NOT
# thinkr:HagensConcept (that subclass requires Hagens' own genuinely
# distinctive coining, per its own class definition — this doesn't
# meet that bar honestly, however real and recurring the pattern is).
#############################################

tgs:Concept.DistributedAgency a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Distributed Agency, Not Saviors"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, recurring pattern across independently-recorded episodes: meaningful change comes from ordinary, distributed action rather than a singular hero or savior figure. Direct textual evidence spans multiple episodes, not one coined term: Frankly_58's own real closing reframe (Sam and Gollum, not Frodo, as the actual Catalysts who changed Lord of the Rings' outcome — 'you never know when your actions could shift the larger story'); Frankly_132's direct claim that reluctance to lead 'might be a good indication that you could be a good leader tomorrow,' paired with its critique that modern systems select for Dark Triad traits in those who actively seek leadership; RR-17's two guests independently converging on 'find like-minded people close to home' as their practical closing advice, rather than pointing toward a movement or a leader; and the real, structural logic of Buckminster Fuller's trim-tab principle (small, precisely-placed, unglamorous leverage redirecting something enormous), which is also the real source of Priscilla Trịnh's own 'Trintab' nickname from RR-9."@en ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:scopeNote "Identified and named as a real pattern by MJSullivan during this session, not something Hagens has explicitly unified under one term himself — worth being honest about that distinction even though the pattern itself is real and evidence-grounded across genuinely independent sources."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# added 2026-08-24 
#############################################

tgs:Concept.PersonalAgency a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Personal Agency"@en ;
    skos:definition "The lived experience that some part of one's life, attention, and time is genuinely one's own to choose — distinct from tgs:Concept.DistributedAgency (a collective/systemic pattern about who drives change). Frankly_129's own organizing term, scientifically grounded via learned-helplessness/self-efficacy research (the nervous system learning whether effort produces outcomes). Frankly_142 directly ties it to dread: 'dread is the experience of being acted upon by a future you cannot influence, and agency is the opposite stance.'"@en ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:scopeNote "NOT to be confused with the existing tgs:Concept.DistributedAgency ('Distributed Agency, Not Saviors') — that concept is about collective action replacing savior-figures; this one is the individual psychological/nervous-system capacity the Staying Human series' own Part 1 is entirely about."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.Dread a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Dread"@en ;
    skos:definition "A physiological body state, not merely a feeling — chronic anticipatory threat-response activation (amygdala/HPA-axis/cortisol) triggered by slow-moving, abstract, unresolvable risk the nervous system cannot discharge through fight-or-flight. Frankly_142's own central subject; grounded in real research on 'extreme dreaders' (~28% of subjects who preferred immediate stronger pain over waiting for milder pain) and climate psychiatrist Lise Van Susteren's 'pre-traumatic stress.'"@en ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:scopeNote "Directly cross-referenced in Frankly_143 as 'full dread... viewed from a slightly different angle' from tgs:Concept.FutureCapture — the two are presented as the same underlying phenomenon (DMN dominance with civilizational content) described from two angles, not two separate claims, matching this graph's existing 'two facets, not two concepts' pattern (see Concept.NarrativeAsActiveInference)."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.Presence a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Presence"@en ;
    skos:definition "Frankly_143's own central subject: full attentional engagement with the present moment, framed as the destination dread transforms into 'when fully metabolized' (per Frankly_142's own closing) and as the ground from which any real response to the metacrisis must be taken."@en ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real prior art cited directly in the transcript across contemplative traditions (Thich Nhat Hanh, the Christian contemplative 'sacrament of the present moment') and neuroscience (default mode network research) — treated here as one Concept with converging support, not split by tradition."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.DefaultModeNetwork a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Default Mode Network"@en ;
    skos:definition "The brain-region network active during self-referential processing, theory of mind, mind-wandering, and mental time travel — anti-correlated with the task-positive network (TPN), which handles direct engaged attention. Chronic DMN dominance is measurably linked to reduced hippocampal grey matter, higher anxiety/depression, and diminished sensory processing."@en ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    skos:scopeNote "Only the graph's second real citation of DMN research — tgs:Interview.TGS_191_TaylorGuthrie is the first, explicitly named in Frankly_143's own transcript as 'the only prior TGS coverage.' The Task Positive Network (TPN), DMN's real anti-correlated partner, is deliberately folded into this definition rather than minted separately this pass."@en ;
    .

tgs:Concept.FutureCapture a thinkr:Concept, thinkr:HagensConcept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Future Capture"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own coined term (Frankly_143): the state where 'the future is so loud and so vivid and so saturated with implications... that the present just can't compete with it' — the future becomes 'a place we live' rather than visit, and the present becomes 'a kind of waiting room.' Explicitly framed as the same underlying phenomenon as tgs:Concept.Dread, viewed from the angle of what it does to attention rather than what it feels like."@en ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    .

tgs:Concept.EcologicalAwakening a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ecological Awakening"@en ;
    skos:definition "Plotkin's own central term, and this episode's own title concept: a felt, experiential (not merely intellectual) shift from experiencing an 'invisible wall' between oneself and the rest of the living world to experiencing kinship with all of it — 'the earth community' — often accompanied by real grief for the years lived without that awareness. Plotkin estimates roughly 20-25% of people have had this experience. Distinct from taking a class in ecology: cognitive understanding without the felt shift doesn't count."@en ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    skos:scopeNote "Direct evidence this recurs beyond one episode: Plotkin himself ties it explicitly to Joanna Macy's Great Turning (third dimension — a shift in values and consciousness) within the same conversation."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.BillPlotkin ;
    .

tgs:Concept.EcocentricDevelopmentalWheel a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ecocentric (Soulcentric) Developmental Wheel"@en ;
    skos:definition "Plotkin's eight-stage model of healthy human development, each stage paired with a human archetype and an earth archetype: (1) Innocent in the Nest — early childhood; (2) Explorer in the Garden — middle childhood; (3) Thespian at the Oasis — early adolescence; (4) Wanderer in the Cocoon — late adolescence, the stage of soul initiation, which 'most contemporary people never reach'; (5) Apprentice at the Wellspring — early adulthood; (6) Artisan in the Wild Orchard — late adulthood; (7) Master in the Grove of Elders — early elderhood; (8) Sage in the Mountain Cave — late elderhood. Explicitly contrasted with an 'egocentric wheel' modeling the stages most contemporary people actually go through instead, most of them stalled in an unhealthy version of stage 3."@en ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    skos:scopeNote "Plotkin's own real prior art cited as convergent, non-Western evidence for this model: Malidoma Somé's account of Dagara initiation rites, Martín Prechtel's account of Tzutujil Maya culture, and Gregory Cajete's writing on Native American educational traditions — none of the three built as Personas this pass."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.BillPlotkin ;
    .

tgs:Concept.ArrestedHumanDevelopment a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Arrested Human Development"@en ;
    skos:definition "Plotkin's own direct causal claim, stated as this episode's central thesis: 'the root cause [of the metacrisis] is arrested human development' — also termed 'systemic human development oppression.' Distinct from tgs:Concept.Metacrisis itself (the pattern of interconnected crises) by proposing a specific generative mechanism behind it, located in human developmental psychology rather than economic or ecological systems directly."@en ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.Metacrisis ;
    skos:scopeNote "A real, single-episode claim — not yet independently corroborated elsewhere in this graph. Flagged for revisiting if it recurs, matching this graph's own recurrence-based minting discipline."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.BillPlotkin ;
    .

tgs:Concept.GreatTurning a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Great Turning"@en ;
    skos:definition "Joanna Macy's framework, explained by Plotkin in real depth (not a passing citation): the shift from an 'Industrial Growth Society' toward a 'life-sustaining civilization,' unfolding across three simultaneous dimensions — (1) holding actions to save as much life as possible now, (2) building the structural and technical foundations for healthier systems (food, energy, governance), and (3) a shift in consciousness and values, which Plotkin explicitly connects back to tgs:Concept.EcologicalAwakening and his own developmental wheel. Macy pairs this with 'the Great Unraveling' — the ongoing collapse of existing systems — as two faces of the same real historical moment, not opposed processes."@en ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsFrameworks ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real external source, not a Hagens or Plotkin coinage — Joanna Macy is cited directly by name throughout as its originator. Macy herself not yet built as a Persona in this graph (cited here and, separately, in Frankly_142's own scopeNote as a deferred candidate)."@en ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.JoannaMacy ;
    .

# added 2026-08-25

tgs:Concept.SupplyChainFragility a thinkr:Concept, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Supply Chain Fragility / Pinch Points"@en ;
    skos:definition "Modern global supply chains are far more concentrated, specialized, and fragile than they appear — a small number of hyper-specialized, often invisible 'pinch points' (a single factory, a single region, a single material) can be genuinely load-bearing for enormous downstream systems, disproportionate to their own small economic footprint. Real evidenced cases: the 1915 UK 'Glass Famine' (an entire domestic industry allowed to wither, then found existentially necessary within a few years, per tgs:Interview.TGS_127_EdConway); a single Birmingham factory supplying roughly half the world's automotive rearview-mirror electrodes; the 2011 Fukushima earthquake halting Ford's US truck production over a single specialized paint pigment. The mechanism Ed Conway identifies: modern comparative-advantage-driven specialization increases aggregate efficiency while increasing structural fragility — 'you have these factories which are uber-specialised... and they're pinch points. Until those pinch points explode, you don't really know about it.'"@en ;
    dcterms:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsFrameworks ;
    thinkr:influencedBy tgs:Persona.EdConway ;
    thinkr:relatesTo tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, deliberate general-purpose applicability, per MJSullivan's own stated reason for selecting this episode 2026-08-24: this concept is a candidate response/caveat to a wide range of articles about growth, consumption, or globalized production, not narrowly scoped to energy/climate topics — directly relevant to the tng-app design work's 'systemic reframing' step from this same session."@en ;
    .